{{- /* The Codex plugin's hooks.json body, rendered.

       It lives here rather than only in the plugin tree because three consumers
       must agree on the SAME rendered bytes:

         - dot_local/share/dotfiles-codex-plugin/hooks/hooks.json.tmpl, the file
           Codex actually reads;
         - codex-hook-trust.tmpl, which attests that declaration — a record
           computed from different bytes is indistinguishable from no record,
           and the hook then stops running with no session-visible symptom;
         - the plugin's plugin.json.tmpl version digest, which must move when
           the declaration changes or the harness keeps serving a cached tree.

       The command is an absolute path resolved at render time, so the hook
       needs no runtime variable expansion to find the binary.

       It stays a command STRING rather than moving to an exec-form `args`
       array. codex-hook-trust.tmpl normalizes `type`, `command`, `timeout`,
       `async`, `statusMessage` and `additionalContextLimit` and has no `args`
       member, so an `args` key would sit outside the hashed identity — the
       silent-untrust failure the record exists to prevent. Whether Codex even
       accepts exec form is unsettled; adopting it here means extending that
       normalization in the same change.

       The PreToolUse entry is the launch gate: it denies a shell launch of
       codex, claude or omp while Orca manages the session.

       It carries NO matcher, deliberately. A matcher here would filter on the
       shell tool's name, and that name is not stable: rust-v0.154.0 ships its
       shell handler as `exec_command` under `unified_exec` and carries no
       `shell` handler at all, so a matcher written against today's name would
       silently stop matching after an upgrade — and a gate that never fires is
       indistinguishable from one with nothing to deny. Without a matcher the
       hook runs on every tool call and the guard decides, which costs a process
       spawn per call and is the price of not going quietly inert. Add a matcher
       only once a captured Codex PreToolUse event pins the tool name, and pin
       that name in the fixture in the same change.

       Adding this event does not disturb the SessionStart trust record.
       codex-hook-trust.tmpl indexes group and handler positionally WITHIN one
       event (`…:<event>:<group>:<handler>`), so a new event key gets its own
       record and `…:session_start:0:0` keeps both its key and its hash. The
       trust template already maps PreToolUse to `pre_tool_use` and already
       lists that event as honouring additionalContextLimit.

       Takes the chezmoi context as its argument. */ -}}
{{- $binary := joinPath .chezmoi.homeDir ".local/libexec/orchestration-hook" -}}
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": {{ printf "%s guard --harness codex" $binary | quote }},
            "timeout": 10,
            "additionalContextLimit": 0
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "SessionStart": [
      {
        "matcher": "startup|resume|clear|compact",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": {{ printf "%s hook --harness codex" $binary | quote }},
            "timeout": 15,
            "additionalContextLimit": 0
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
